   "id": "s01",
   "label": "Today",
   "title": "An archipelago between two continents",
   "text": "Indonesia is a chain of some seventeen thousand islands between mainland Asia and Australia. Reid describes the region as lands joined, not split, by the sea and timed by the monsoon winds, so outsiders arrived along a few great routes from India and China. Ananta and colleagues count over six hundred ethnic groups in the 2010 census. Sumba sits south of these routes, beyond the busy straits of Java and Sumatra.\n\nMore: Speaking notes: about 17,000 islands; over 600 ethnic groups (check Ananta's exact figure, 633 or 630, before saying it aloud).",
