   "id": "s05",
   "label": "1290s – 1660s",
   "title": "The Islamic layer",
   "text": "Van Vollenhoven places the Islamic contribution to adat in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. Ricklefs traces Islam from Pasai in north Sumatra, and Reid shows it spreading with trade through Melaka and the ports of Java's north coast. Gowa-Tallo in Makassar accepted Islam in 1605. Sumba met this wave on its shoreline, through traders from Makassar and Bugis lands, while the interior kept its own ancestral religion.",
