   "id": "s10",
   "label": "1945 – 1998",
   "title": "One nation, one centre",
   "text": "Elson traces how the idea of Indonesia took over the Dutch colonial territory whole. Ananta and colleagues show the Javanese as by far the largest group, and Java held the capital. Vel writes that from 1965 Sumba was ruled by a bureaucracy directed from Jakarta, which brought schools and roads and required everyone to belong to one of five recognised religions. Marapu was not one. Robinson describes the killings of 1965 that made that requirement dangerous.\n\nMore: Eva's point for this slide: the new nation-state reproduced the colonial structure (a centre on Java governing a periphery), which is different from settler colonialism as in Australia, where the colonisers stayed as the majority.",
